# 1qb-DB waveform demo

This directory connects the analytic Mathematica model to the sampled
waveforms. [`1qb-DB-demo.ipynb`](1qb-DB-demo.ipynb) is complete and has been
validated from a clean Python namespace.

The numerical propagation, plotting, reporting, and validation functions live
in [`db_helpers.py`](db_helpers.py), keeping the notebook cells short.

## Two notebooks

| notebook | model | helper module |
|---|---|---|
| [`1qb-DB-demo.ipynb`](1qb-DB-demo.ipynb) | two-level, dimensionless | `db_helpers.py` |
| [`1qb-DB-transmon.ipynb`](1qb-DB-transmon.ipynb) | three-level transmon, laboratory units | `db_transmon.py` |

The transmon notebook reuses the same waveform CSVs and the same error-curve
machinery, and adds what a two-level picture cannot express: leakage to
$\lvert2\rangle$ and a physical gate duration. It fixes the remaining gauge
freedom by driving every pulse at the same peak Rabi rate, so the cost of
robustness shows up as gate time. New candidates it optimizes are written to
`transmon_waveforms/` by
[`generate_transmon_waveforms.py`](generate_transmon_waveforms.py); the
checksum-managed store in `../X_gate_pulse_generation/waveforms/` is read-only
from here.

## Inputs

Import from
[`../X_gate_pulse_generation/waveforms/`](../X_gate_pulse_generation/waveforms/):

- `gaussian_x_pi.csv`;
- `resonant_composite_robust_x_pi.csv`;
- `metadata.json`;
- `barq_resonant_screening_x_pi.csv`, used only in the error-curve comparison
  of section 1 as a smooth reference, never in the DB sections.

All of these use the same normalized convention and have zero detuning.

## Notebook contents

1. Plot \(\Omega_x(t)\) and \(\Omega_y(t)\).
2. Plot the amplitude-error curve \(R(t)\) of both controls, plus one smooth
   resonant reference waveform, in 3D and in the three plane projections, and
   report the closure distance \(|R(T_g)-R(0)|\) with the signed projected
   areas.
3. Verify that both pulses implement \(X_\pi\) at zero error.
4. Inject \(\epsilon\) into the sampled Hamiltonian and numerically calculate
   the time-ordered noisy gate \(U_\epsilon(T_g)\).
5. Use the calculated gate to simulate \(n=0,\ldots,60\) and
   \(\epsilon=\pm3\%\).
6. Compare gate fidelity and \(\Delta P_0\).

The DB curves are calculated from the waveform Hamiltonians rather than an
analytic pulse-area formula. The results reproduce the validation values
recorded in `metadata.json`. Run all cells using the `curve` environment.

## Transmon error model (transmon notebook, section 2)

Fixed once in `db_transmon.TransmonDevice`, never swept:

| term | value | enters as |
|---|---|---|
| anharmonicity | \(\alpha/2\pi=-200\) MHz | \(\alpha\lvert2\rangle\langle2\rvert\) |
| peak drive | \(\Omega_{\max}/2\pi=50\) MHz | gauge fixing; sets each \(T_g\) |
| relaxation | \(T_1=60\) µs | \(\sqrt{\Gamma_1}\,a\), \(a=\lvert0\rangle\langle1\rvert+\sqrt2\lvert1\rangle\langle2\rvert\) |
| pure dephasing | \(T_2^{\rm echo}=60\) µs | \(\sqrt{2\Gamma_\phi}\,\hat n\), \(\Gamma_\phi=1/T_2-1/2T_1\) |
| static detuning | \(\delta_z/2\pi=0.1\) MHz | \(-\delta_z\hat n\); quasi-static, partly echoed by the sequence |
| control error | \(\epsilon=\pm3\%\) | \((\Omega_x,\Omega_y)\to(1+\epsilon)(\Omega_x,\Omega_y)\) |

The Lindblad \(T_2\) and the static \(\delta_z\) are separate channels on
purpose: only the second one is coherent, and only the second one is the channel
whose geometry the error curves above describe.

One gate is compressed into a single \(9\times9\) quantum channel
(`gate_channel`), so a sequence of \(n\) gates is a matrix power rather than a
re-integration.

**Calibration.** The waveforms are exact \(X(\pi)\) in the two-level model but
not on three levels: at \(\Omega_{\max}/\lvert\alpha\rvert=0.25\) the
\(\lvert2\rangle\) level Stark-shifts the 0–1 transition during the pulse (the
second-order repulsion \(\Omega^2/2\lvert\alpha\rvert\approx6\) MHz) and tilts
the rotation axis out of the equator, by \(0.21\) rad for the Gaussian —
several times the injected \(3\%\). `calibrate` removes this with the three
knobs a laboratory has (drive amplitude, drive frequency, virtual Z), bounded to
\(\pm25\%\) and \(\pm30\) MHz, and minimizes the rotation error *inside* the
qubit subspace with the leakage loss divided out, so that the calibration cannot
exploit the narrow leakage nulls. The injected \(\epsilon\) is applied on top of
the calibrated amplitude. No DRAG is applied anywhere.

## Error-curve geometry

The injected error is multiplicative, so the relevant curve integrates the
toggling-frame drive, \(R(t)=\int_0^t g\,ds\) with
\(U_0^\dagger H_cU_0=\tfrac12g\cdot\vec\sigma\). Its closure distance is the
error rotation angle per unit \(\epsilon\), and its signed area vector
\(\tfrac12\oint R\times dR\) carries the leading second-order term.
`error_curve(pulse, noise="dephasing")` returns the standard unit-speed SCQC
curve instead, for reference.

| curve | length | \(|R(T_g)-R(0)|\) | \(|\vec A|\) | \(\Delta P_0\) at \(\pm3\%\) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Gaussian | \(\pi\) | \(\pi\) | \(0\) | \(1.00\) |
| resonant composite | \(5\pi\) | \(4.70\times10^{-3}\) | \(5.30\times10^{-3}\) | \(3.85\times10^{-6}\) |
| BARQ resonant (smooth, reference) | \(3.43\pi\) | \(2.17\times10^{-1}\) | \(3.73\) | \(2.1\times10^{-2}/1.2\times10^{-1}\) |

The first two curves lie in the \(R_z=0\) plane because \(\Delta\equiv0\) and their
drive axis is piecewise constant; the smooth reference waveform turns its phase
continuously and its curve is genuinely three-dimensional.

The closure ordering matches the DB ordering, which is the point of the panel:
geometry alone ranks the three waveforms before any DB trace is simulated. Note
that the smooth candidate is the *worst* of the three at closing its amplitude
error curve, even though its dephasing curve nearly closes
(\(1.1\times10^{-3}\)) — it is robust against a noise channel this experiment
does not inject. Smoothness of the envelope is not robustness.

The Gaussian curve is a straight line, so its error accumulates at first
order: its angle error is exactly \(\epsilon\pi\) at any \(\epsilon\). The
composite curve almost closes, which is the mechanism behind its flat DB
trace. Its closure reproduces the numerically propagated single-gate angle
error in the \(\epsilon\to0\) limit; at \(\epsilon=\pm3\%\) the propagated
angle is \(3.27\times10^{-5}\), i.e. smaller than the first-order estimate
\(\epsilon|R(T_g)-R(0)|=1.41\times10^{-4}\), because the composite phases were
optimized on the \(\pm3\%\) window rather than for exact closure. Propagating
that single-gate angle through 60 cycles as \(\cos^2(n\theta)\) reproduces the
tabulated \(\Delta P_0=3.85\times10^{-6}\).
